  "source_question": "The source studies which SiNC5H7 products and formation mechanisms are consistent with crossed-beam SiN + isoprene scattering. The task retains that gas-phase reaction and its energetic experimental constraints.",
  "task_question": "Which product structures and formation account are supported for the atomic-hydrogen-loss channel of SiN reacting with isoprene under the supplied single-collision conditions? Establish what is identifiable from a defensible molecular investigation and what alternatives remain unresolved.",
  "scope_mappings": [
    {
      "element": "reactants and observed H-loss channel",
      "source": "main p3 reaction(1), SI p2 TableS1",
      "classification": "experimental identity and conditions"
    },
    {
      "element": "collision/channel energy constraints",
      "source": "main p4 Fig3 and derivation Emax=EC−reaction energy",
      "classification": "experimental observation, not product assignment"
    },
    {
      "element": "author candidate/PES method",
      "source": "main pp3,5–6 Figs4–5; SI p3 energy labels",
      "classification": "PR/private only"
    }
  ],
  "public_facts_and_constraints": "Starting identities are SiN (neutral doublet), isoprene C5H8 (neutral singlet) and atomic H (neutral doublet). The observed heavy-product composition is SiNC5H7; its connectivity and electronic-state assignment are to be investigated. The total neutral reactant system has doublet spin. Conditions are isolated gas-phase single collisions at 25 ± 1 kJ/mol, not thermal solution equilibrium. The measured channel reaction energy is -162 ± 27 kJ/mol relative to separated reactants. Preserve all atoms and physical spin bookkeeping for models derived from these reactants.",
  "agent_decisions": [
    "Select physically admissible product candidates and how to discover or eliminate them.",
    "Choose how to investigate formation and distinguish compatible endpoints from accessible chemistry within the measured energy window.",
    "Choose state/method treatment, search stopping criteria and the strength of the final identification."
  ],
